module

public import FormalConjectures.ErdosProblems.«338»

/-!
# Erdős Problem 338, one variant: the restricted order is bounded in terms of the order

Generated by an AI agent and machine-checked; not reviewed by a human expert.

## Statement

`main` proves the upstream statement `Erdos338.erdos_338.variants.bounded_by_order` (Formal
Conjectures, `FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/338.lean`, commit 9097e2f9, where it is marked
`category research open`), written as `type_of%` of it. The statement is

    ∃ f : ℕ → ℕ, ∀ (A : Set ℕ) (h : ℕ), HasOrder A h →
      (∃ t, HasRestrictedOrder A t) → HasRestrictedOrder A (f h)

`HasOrder A h`: every large integer is a sum of at most `h` elements of `A`, repetition allowed.
`HasRestrictedOrder A t`: every large integer is a sum of at most `t` distinct elements of `A`. The
proof gives the explicit, extremely large value `f h = Q h (typ h h)`. The number `t` plays no role:
the proof only uses that every large integer is a sum of distinct elements of `A`. The axioms used
are `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound` (Lean 4.33.1, Mathlib v4.33.1).

This answers (yes) question 3(ii) of the concluding remarks of Erdős and Graham 1980. It is a
variant of Problem 338, not the headline question 3(i) (necessary and sufficient conditions), and it
says nothing on question 3(iii) (when the two orders are equal) or on sums of exactly `t` distinct
elements. No defect is known in the upstream statement: `HasOrder A h` says that the order is at
most `h`, which gives the same question, and the case `h = 0` is vacuous.

## Outline

A number is called `(k, q)`-robust if it remains a sum of at most `k` distinct elements of `A` when
any `q` numbers are forbidden; `D A k q` is the set of these numbers. `B A r` is the set of sums of
at most `r` elements of `A`, repetition allowed. A scale is an integer `N ≥ 1`. It has type `c` if
at least `N / c` numbers in `[1, 2 N]` are `(2 h, Q h c)`-robust; it may have several types. The
function `Q` is fixed first; the increasing sequence `typ h 0`, `typ h 1`, ... is then built from
`h` and `Q`. "Robust" without numbers is informal.

* Lemma 1 (`add_mem_D`, `sum_mem_D`): robust numbers may be added with repetition if `q` is large,
  since each summand can be written with fresh elements of `A`.
* Lemma 2 (`card_B_succ_le`): up to `N`, at most `r #(B A r)` elements of `B A (r + 1)` need a
  repeated summand (representations with the largest number of distinct summands).
* Lemma 3 (`dense_D`): if `B A (r + 1)` is dense up to `N` and `B A r` is very sparse, then many
  numbers in `[1, 2 N]` are robust (count pairs).
* Lemma 4 (`exists_type`): so every large `N` has a type at most `typ h h`, which depends only on
  `h`.
* Lemma 5 (`cover`): if `T` has at least `N / c` elements in `[1, 2 N]`, then the multiples of
  `g = g c` in `[4 g N, 10 g N]` are sums of at most `K c` elements of `T` (two translates meet
  modulo a popular sum of two elements).
* Lemma 6 (`exists_residue`): each residue modulo `d` is a sum of fewer than `d` distinct elements
  of `A` (two initial sums agree modulo `d`). `g c` and `K c` do not involve `Q`, and `Q h c` is
  large compared with both, so robust numbers can avoid these elements when `d = g c`.
* Lemma 7 (`one_scale`): at a scale `N` of type `c`, the multiples of `g = g c` in `[4 g N, 10 g N]`
  are sums of boundedly many distinct elements of `A` outside any given set of `g` numbers.
* Lemma 8 (`two_scales`): if `N + 1` has type `c'`, the same holds up to `5 g' (N + 1)`, where
  `g' = g c'`. Beyond `10 g N` we have `c' > c`, and copies of one large number that is
  `(2 h, Q h c')`-robust are subtracted.
* The theorem (`bounded_by_order`, `main`): take the scale `N` with `5 g N ≤ n < 5 g' (N + 1)`, fix
  the residue of `n` modulo `g`, and apply Lemma 8 to the rest.

## Priority

New as far as known: two searches up to 25 Nov 2026 found no published or public statement or proof,
formal or informal, of a bound that depends only on `h`. The searches had gaps (see below), so this
means likely new, not certainly new. The claim is only about the uniformity in `A`. The strategy is
that of [CYZ], and the uniform bound should not be described as out of reach of their method.

* [CYZ]: S.-Q. Chen, Q.-H. Yang and L. Zhao, "On a problem of Erdős and Graham related to restricted
  order", https://github.com/csq20180327/Erdos338, public since 18 Nov 2026 (commits c1b4e5d and
  2e7fc3d), revision of 20 Nov 2026 (commit 8b9b7a0, still the latest on 25 Nov). Unrefereed, not
  found on arXiv.

[CYZ] proves that a basis has a restricted order if and only if sums of its distinct elements reach
every residue class modulo every `q` (Theorem 2), and that this holds if `A` minus any finite set is
still a basis (Theorem 1). Priority for these results is theirs; they are not proved in this file.
A longer file by the same agents, not included in this repository, proves them in Lean as well, and
also the bound for bases that are complete modulo every `q`.

[CYZ] states no bound in terms of the order: in its Theorem 3 the number `H`, and in its Lemma 4 the
modulus `g`, depend on `A`. However, its proofs appear to give a bound in terms of `h`, without an
explicit value, in three steps (checked by hand only, by AI agents, and not found in print): (1) the
lower density of the sums of at most `h` distinct elements is bounded below in terms of `h`; (2)
with this and the full form of Kneser's theorem (Math. Z. 58 (1953) 459-484, p. 463, as quoted in
https://mathoverflow.net/questions/484732), `g` and `K` in Lemma 4 of [CYZ] are bounded in terms of
`h` and `b`; (3) a sequence of bases of order at most `h` with restricted orders tending to infinity
is excluded by the proof of Theorem 3 of [CYZ] in a limit over the profinite integers, with Baire's
theorem. The proof in this file uses neither Kneser's theorem nor a limit or compactness argument,
and it does not need Theorem 3 of [CYZ].

## Source and earlier results

* P. Erdős and R. L. Graham, "On bases with an exact order", Acta Arith. 37 (1980) 201-207, p. 206,
  remark 3(i)-(iv), https://static.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1980-22.pdf.
* N. Hegyvári, F. Hennecart and A. Plagne, "Answer to a question by Burr and Erdős on restricted
  addition, and related results", Combin. Probab. Comput. 16 (2007) 747-756,
  https://www.cmls.polytechnique.fr/perso/plagne/Erdos-Burr.pdf, p. 3: ask for a bound in terms of
  `h`. For the least possible bound `f*(h)` (written `f(h)` there; it is not the `f h` of this file)
  they record `f*(2) = 4` and prove `f*(h) ≥ 2^(h-2) + h - 1` and nothing from above for `h ≥ 3`.
  Their sets `A` with unbounded gaps between sums of at most `h` distinct elements do not contradict
  `main`, which allows more than `h` summands. Bateman's basis `{1} ∪ h ℕ`, `h ≥ 3`, has no
  restricted order, so `main` says nothing about it.
* Order two: J. B. Kelly, "Restricted bases", Amer. J. Math. 79 (1957) 258-264; F. Hennecart, "On
  the restricted order of asymptotic bases of order two", Ramanujan J. 9 (2005) 123-130.
* D. Veljjanoski, "Erdős problem #338 — partial results on restricted order", 1 Oct 2026,
  https://github.com/veljjanoski/erdos338: Theorem 1 of [CYZ] for sets of positive lower density,
  with a bound depending on the density. Lean proof (existence only), 2 Nov 2026:
  https://github.com/veljjanoski/erdos338-palomar (PALOMAR-2026-11-03-000016).
* https://erdosproblemaday.com/report/338 (and 15, 17 Nov 2026): bounds in terms of the density.
* P. Erdős, M. B. Nathanson and A. Sárközy, "Sumsets containing infinite arithmetic progressions",
  J. Number Theory 28 (1988) 159-166, https://static.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1988-33.pdf: sets of positive
  lower density; a basis need not have positive density.

## Credits for ideas

The overall strategy is that of [CYZ], which was available to the agents that produced this proof.
Earlier instances of it, for sets of positive lower density, are in Veljjanoski's note of 1 Oct
2026: numbers with many representations found by counting pairs, Kneser's theorem, fixing the
residue, greedy disjoint representations.

* Lemma 1: the ordered greedy choice of disjoint sets in the proof of Lemma 4 of [CYZ]. Veljjanoski
  credits the disjoint-representation step to Hegyvári, Hennecart and Plagne 2007 (proof of Theorem
  9 in the preprint).
* Lemma 2: iterated, it gives again a theorem of N. Hegyvári, F. Hennecart and A. Plagne, "A proof
  of two Erdős' conjectures on restricted addition and further results", J. reine angew. Math. 560
  (2003) 199-220 (Theorem 4; Erdős Problem 339), in the form of Lemma 2 of [CYZ] (positive lower
  density of the sums of at most `h` distinct elements) and with a constant depending only on `h`.
  That paper was not available, so the proofs were not compared. Public Lean proof with such a
  constant: https://github.com/plby/lean-proofs (src/latest/ErdosProblems/Erdos339.lean).
* Lemma 3: Lemma 3 of [CYZ] and the density estimate in the proof of Lemma 4 there. Here `D` asks
  for a representation avoiding any `q` numbers, instead of `q` disjoint representations.
* Lemma 4: the least `r` with a dense `R_r` and a hierarchy of constants, in Lemma 4 of [CYZ]. There
  `R_r` is the set of sums of at most `r` distinct elements of `A`; here `B A r` allows repetition.
* Lemma 5 replaces Kneser's theorem in [CYZ]. Reducing a dense set modulo one number and adding
  copies of that number also occurs in `Covering.lean` of erdos338-palomar, there modulo the largest
  element and with Kneser's theorem for finite abelian groups.
* Lemma 6: the prefix-sum pigeonhole, as in the proof of Theorem 3 of [CYZ].
* The last step, fixing the residue first, is in the proof of Theorem 2 of [CYZ].

What is added here is the uniformity in `A`: the number `Q h c` of forbidden elements depends on the
type `c` of the scale and is chosen after the modulus `g c`, and two consecutive scales of different
types are compared (Lemma 8). This and the Lean proof were produced by AI agents in the run that
produced this file.

